## Claim
Text: "More than 225,000 people dead, 225,000. The estimates are, if we’d have acted responsibly, there’d be 160,000 fewer dead than there are today, because of covid-19."
Claim author: Joe Biden
Claim date: October 30, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### What was the officially reported cumulative number of deaths attributed to COVID-19 in the United States on or before October 30, 2020?
Answer: As of September 19, 2020, the United States had officially reported a total of 198,589 COVID-19 deaths.

Source URL: https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2771841

### Which specific source (e.g., Johns Hopkins University, CDC, Worldometer) was the most commonly cited authoritative tracker for the U.S. COVID-19 death count on October 30, 2020?
Answer: On October 30, 2020, the most commonly cited authoritative tracker for the U.S. COVID-19 death count was the Center for Systems Science and Engineering at Johns Hopkins University, which is noted in the source as a key data provider.

Source URL: https://injuryfacts.nsc.org/home-and-community/safety-topics/covid-19-cases-in-the-united-states/

### What specific definition or benchmark of "acting responsibly" is used in the estimate cited by Joe Biden regarding potential COVID-19 deaths in the United States?
Answer: The search result does not provide the specific definition or benchmark of "acting responsibly" as referenced in Joe Biden's estimate, as it focuses on general policy response metrics and does not cite his claim or its source.

Source URL: https://ourworldindata.org/policy-responses-covid

### Which specific study, model, or report provides the estimate that 160,000 fewer people would have died from COVID-19 in the United States by late October 2020 under different circumstances?
Answer: The estimate that 160,000 fewer people would have died is derived from a report by Columbia University’s National Center for Disaster Preparedness, titled “130,000 – 210,000 Avoidable COVID-19 Deaths — and Counting — in the U.S.,” which compared U.S. outcomes to six other high-income countries and found that if the U.S. had followed similar policies, it could have avoided between 130,000 and 210,000 deaths by mid-October 2020.

Source URL: https://news.climate.columbia.edu/2020/10/22/covid-19-response-avoidable-deaths/

### What were the key assumptions and methodological parameters of the study or model that produced the estimate of 160,000 potentially averted COVID-19 deaths in the United States?
Answer: The estimate of 160,000 averted deaths, which falls within the 130,000–210,000 range cited in a Columbia University report, was derived using a comparative analysis of COVID-19 mortality rates per 100,000 population between the U.S. and six other high-income countries (South Korea, Japan, Australia, Germany, France, and Canada). The core assumption was that "if the U.S. had followed [the] policies and protocols" of these nations, it could have achieved a similar per-capita mortality rate; the methodology involved calculating the deaths each country would have had if its population were the size of the U.S. and subtracting that from actual U.S. deaths. The report acknowledges limitations, noting this "crude mortality estimate" does not fully account for factors like demographics or health risks, though it argues these do not explain the disparity.

Source URL: https://ncdp.columbia.edu/custom-content/uploads/2020/10/Avoidable-COVID-19-Deaths-US-NCDP.pdf

