### On what date was the study or model providing the "160,000 fewer deaths" estimate published or released?
Answer: The study providing the "160,000 fewer deaths" estimate was published on October 22, 2020, as part of a Columbia University report that found between 130,000 and 210,000 deaths could have been avoided.

Source URL: https://news.climate.columbia.edu/2020/10/22/covid-19-response-avoidable-deaths/

### How did the actual U.S. COVID-19 death toll on October 30, 2020, compare to the death tolls of other large, wealthy nations per capita on the same date?
Answer: On October 30, 2020, the U.S. COVID-19 per capita death toll was among the highest of large, wealthy nations, a trend that had been consistent since spring. As of September 19, 2020 (just six weeks prior), the U.S. rate was 60.3 per 100,000, higher than countries like Canada (24.6) and comparable to hard-hit nations like Italy (59.1), but the U.S. rate continued to climb faster than even those high-mortality countries after May 2020.

Source URL: https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2771841

### What were the major U.S. federal and state policy actions regarding COVID-19 (e.g., lockdowns, mask mandates, testing strategy) in the period between the start of the pandemic and October 2020 that might be evaluated under the term "acted responsibly"?
Answer: - **Leadership shifts** created confusion, with HHS, VP Pence's Task Force, and FEMA all holding lead roles within three months, and the March 13 emergency declaration and March 19 FEMA takeover occurring only after cases had surged to tens of thousands per day.

Source URL: https://www.hsgac.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/imo/media/doc/221208_HSGACMajorityReport_Covid-19.pdf

### Have other public health experts or institutions, besides the one cited, published analyses with similar estimates of preventable U.S. COVID-19 deaths by late 2020?
Answer: Yes, other public health experts and institutions published similar estimates; for example, the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation (IHME) at the University of Washington was cited in the same Columbia University report, which stated that their analysis estimated earlier interventions "could have prevented as much as 90% of deaths in the U.S. through April 14th if implemented just two weeks earlier."

Source URL: https://ncdp.columbia.edu/custom-content/uploads/2020/10/Avoidable-COVID-19-Deaths-US-NCDP.pdf

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the fact-check confirms that as of October 30, 2020, the United States had surpassed 225,000 COVID-19 deaths, aligning with the first part of the claim. The specific estimate of "160,000 fewer dead" is directly traceable to a Columbia University report published just days prior, which estimated a range of 130,000 to 210,000 avoidable deaths based on comparing U.S. outcomes to other high-income nations under the assumption of following their policies—a plausible interpretation of "acted responsibly."

The evidence is sufficiently clear to make a determination. The factual accuracy of the death toll is supported, and the cited estimate comes directly from a specific, contemporary study. While the phrase "acted responsibly" in the claim is general, the evidence links it to a well-defined comparative analysis.

Conclusion: The recorded evidence directly verifies the factual elements of the claim—the death count and the source of the specific estimate—which supports the statement. Therefore, the claim is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The claim is supported; by October 30, 2020, the U.S. COVID-19 death toll had surpassed 225,000, and the estimate of 160,000 fewer deaths is directly linked to a [Columbia University report](https://news.climate.columbia.edu/2020/10/22/covid-19-response-avoidable-deaths/) published on October 22, 2020, which used a comparative analysis of per-capita mortality rates between the U.S. and six other high-income countries to conclude that between 130,000 and 210,000 deaths were avoidable under different policy assumptions.